  "description": "T4 Ailanthus TPO 1/1961 \r\nT4 Ailanthus: Crown reduce by 2-3m and remove any dead, rubbing or crossing branches. Thin by 20% whilst maintaining its natural shape. Reason: Routine maintenance\r\nThe request for a 2 to 3 metre reduction is made on arboricultural management grounds rather than amenity reduction. The tree is a not yet mature and fast growing specimen located within a confined garden flower bed, in close proximity to surrounding planting and garden features. Ailanthus is known for vigorous extension growth and a strong tendency to outgrow restricted spaces quickly if not managed early.\r\nThe proposed reduction is intended as an early formative and size management intervention to control height and spread at a stage where pruning wounds will remain relatively small and well within the tree's capacity to respond. Managing the tree at this stage will help avoid the need for more severe intervention in the future, which could result in larger wounds, increased stress, and a greater risk of poor wound occlusion.\r\nIn its current position, unchecked growth is likely to lead to future conflict with the surrounding garden layout and neighbouring structures. A modest reduction now will help maintain the tree at a scale appropriate to its setting while retaining its long term health, structure, and contribution to local amenity.\r\nThe works would be undertaken sympathetically, retaining a natural form and avoiding heavy or repeated pruning cycles later in the tree's life.\r\n",
